d that and  to put that money into the schools that  support the vast majority of our  children you've talked about the  pressures that we're under whether it's  inflation but also of course a borrowing  and borrowing costs  um are there any areas of public  spending where you think actually yes  this is where we could look at finding  spend savings  I think it's quite tough I mean there  has been a big squeeze over the last 12  years I mean we do believe that you need  to make sure you're getting value for  money that's why we've said that labor  would set up the office of value for  money to make sure that we're getting  real value from everything any areas  where you think that look maybe this if  we have to make difficult choices this  is the department where maybe they could  fall  um there will you know no government is  perfect in how it spends its money and  of course you should be looking at  getting the best out of the money that  you're spending but I don't think if I  look for example across education it's  not that they are Awash with money and  have got more money than they know what  to do with I mean unlike the oil and gas  companies who haven't actually been  taxed for what they have been enjoying  at the moment so I think all of this  comes down to choices for government  both in terms of priorities but also tax  fairness that is the key though isn't  the choice as the government which I  guess is why I'm asking you know if  there is any areas where you think  savings could be found well I'd always  be up to looking for it but  I think given the pressures that our  public sector has been under these last  12 years whether it's our schools or  hospitals an awful lot has been cut back  and cut back to such a point that for  example with Schools they're the last  line of defense really for so many of  our communities that wider  infrastructure that wider support that  families need that's gone and so much of  it falls to school so actually if you  had to free up if you get high standards  and excellence in all of our schools you  could deliver that far more effectively  if you had that wider support available  to All Families so that doesn't always  just brought to our teachers and school  leaders and I want to ask you about  dominant Robb who obviously is facing  allegations of bullying and there's  going to be in independent investigation  into his conduct he says he welcomes it  he'll comply are you satisfied with that  well we don't know who as yet is to lead  this independent inquiry of course the  Prime Minister hasn't appointed his  independent ethics advisor he promised  us a government that would be rooted in  Integrity professionalism and  accountability this Falls a long way  short but it's not just about about  Dominic Robb you look at sowella  bravaman back in the cabinet having  breached the ministerial court on an  issue of National Security Gavin  Williamson put back into government I  think this speaks to a deeper question  as to Rishi seen next character and also  his weakness that he feels so weak  within his own party that he is doing  what he feels is right by his party it  should be about what's right for the  country that isn't what's happening  right now I just wonder if you think it  also speaks to a deeper problem in  Westminster and like trying to sort of  take the kind of Party politics out of  this um you know Sky News has a podcast  where two of our reporters have been  looking into allegations of bullying the  Open Secret podcast and they found  examples across all parties one as staff  are telling them I'm sure every staffer  in Parliament has got some a story even  if they weren't bullied they know  someone who was I mean is there a  problem with bullying and the treatment  of Staff in Westminster I think you get  uh you know real problems where you have  an imbalance of power and where people  don't feel able to make complaints a map  for too long has been the case in  Westminster and you know we do have a  process now and I hope that people will  come forward where they have been on the  end of poor treatment in the workplace  because no matter where you work you  should be treated with respect and you  shouldn't be subjected to bullying and  the same is true of course right across  government as well and sometimes people  don't feel comfortable coming forward  they want to try and just plow on and do  their best but no one should feel under  that kind of pressure and I think you do  see what happens when you have a more  open culture where people do feel that  they can come forward and you know we've  got more work to do in Westminster  absolutely but I think it does come down  to that that Dynamic around the  imbalance of power and you know we all  of us as politicians have a  responsibility to make sure that you  know we are respectful of others you  know politics can involve you know  robust exchange that's as it should be  but you don't cross that line thank you  very much for being on the program thank  you
 

